# Badges G–I: checked against official scouting.org excerpts.

@owner mb.geocaching
@complete partial
@note Excerpts confirmed requirements 1 and 7–9; requirements 2–6 cover etiquette, terms and navigation but their exact numbering was not visible, so they are not outlined yet.
@desc Geocaching combines navigation and outdoor ethics: safety and planning, cache etiquette, GPS and map-and-compass skills, finding caches, Travel Bugs, Cache In Trash Out, and planning a geohunt for younger Scouts.
@safety Geocache only with permission, a buddy and a plan; never bury a cache or hide one where searching could damage the land.
1 k! Safety and planning :: Likely hazards, first aid for common injuries, and planning a GPS outing.
7 a Your first find :: Using a geocaching website, with permission, to find a public cache near you.
8 a A geocaching project :: One option: Cache to Eagle, a Travel Bug, hiding and maintaining a cache, or a Cache In Trash Out event.
9 a Leading a geohunt :: Planning a geohunt with several waypoints for a youth group.

@owner mb.geology
@note Requirements 3–6 were revised for 2026 (including a new careers item), and the excerpts mixed the old and new versions, so no requirement outline is published yet.
@desc Geology is the study of the Earth: what geologists do, reading geologic maps, one in-depth track (surface and sedimentary processes, energy resources, mineral resources, or Earth history and fossils), outdoor ethics while collecting, and careers.
@safety Collect specimens only where it is allowed, wear eye protection when breaking rocks, and stay away from cliffs and unstable slopes.

@owner mb.golf
@note Golf offers a traditional golf option and a disc golf option, each with its own steps; the excerpts did not show their numbering, so no requirement outline is published yet.
@desc Golf can be earned through traditional golf or disc golf: safety and first aid, the rules and etiquette, handicapping or scoring, history, fitness, swing or throwing fundamentals, playing rounds with others, and golf careers.

@owner mb.graphic-arts
@note The 2025 update changed the design requirement to include producing the piece; the excerpts did not show numbering, so no requirement outline is published yet.
@desc Graphic Arts explores how printed and digital communication is made: printing processes, kinds of images, designing a printed piece and producing it, finishing and binding, and visiting a printing or graphics workplace.

@owner mb.health-care-professions
@complete complete
@note Health Care Professions replaced the Medicine merit badge in 2021.
@desc Health Care Professions explores the many careers in medicine and health care (physicians and specialists, nursing, pharmacy and emergency medicine, therapy and nutrition, lab and imaging), plus a workplace visit, medical ethics and privacy, new advances, and volunteering.
1 k Physicians and specialists :: Several professions from the physician and specialist group, their roles and education.
2 k Nursing, pharmacy and EMS :: Several professions from the nursing, pharmacy and emergency medical group.
3 k Therapy and nutrition :: Several professions from the therapy and dietetics group.
4 k Lab, imaging and records :: Several professions from the laboratory, imaging and health records group.
5 a A workplace visit :: Visiting a health care professional at work and discussing their career.
6 d The Physician’s Oath :: What the Physician’s Oath means.
7 k Patient privacy :: Patient confidentiality and HIPAA.
8 k Medical advances :: A recent medical advance and how it could affect patient care.
9 s Volunteering :: Volunteering at a health-related event or facility and reporting what you learned.

@owner mb.home-repairs
@note Requirements 2, 5, 6 and 7 were revised for 2026 and the excerpts grouped tasks by topic rather than number, so no requirement outline is published yet.
@desc Home Repairs builds practical skills for keeping a home in shape: safety, exterior and yard work, shop and carpentry repairs, simple electrical and plumbing tasks, interior repairs, and careers or hobbies that use these skills.
@safety Work with a knowledgeable adult, turn off power and water before repairs, and use eye protection and the right tool for each job.

@owner mb.horsemanship
@note The official A–Z list shows twelve requirements, updated in 2025, but their text was not visible, so no requirement outline is published yet.
@desc Horsemanship teaches safe riding and horse care: safety around horses, breeds and anatomy, health and feeding, grooming and tack, saddling and bridling, and riding skills, under an experienced instructor.
@safety Wear an approved riding helmet and boots, and ride only under a qualified instructor.

@owner mb.insect-study
@complete complete
@note Collect insects only where it is legal; collecting is prohibited in many parks and refuges.
@desc Insect Study introduces the most numerous animals on Earth: safety around stinging insects, anatomy and orders, life cycles, observing and identifying insects, social insects, their role in ecosystems and agriculture, conservation, and careers or hobbies.
@safety Know how to recognize and respond to severe allergic reactions to stings.
1 k! Safety :: Hazards from stinging and biting insects and treating stings, including severe allergic reactions.
2 k Anatomy and orders :: How insects differ from other arthropods, and the major insect orders.
3 a Life cycles :: Complete and incomplete metamorphosis, and raising an insect through its life cycle.
4 a Observing insects :: Observing and identifying live insects across several orders and their roles in ecosystems.
5 a Social insects :: One option: observing an ant colony or a beehive.
6 k Insects in ecosystems :: Insects in food webs and as pollinators.
7 k Insects and people :: Bees and colony collapse, and alternatives to insecticides.
8 k Conservation :: An insect migration and a threatened or endangered insect.
9 a Careers or hobbies :: One option: an insect-related career, or a hobby.
